  "full_text": "So there was a there's a Monkey Nature documentary and it shows this monkey with a bunch of headaches from Trying to manage the harem. Oh Don't have a hair on my die. I got headaches anyway So there's there's perfect justice, right? Anyway, let go and relax. By wisdom a house is built. By understanding it is established. The older I get, the more I appreciate the benefits of taking time. Woodwork done slowly and meticulously by craftsmen is beautiful and able to endure the test of time. Art, whether musical composition, needlework, sculpture, or painting, requires time and attention to detail. Even the cultivation of our walk with God requires a great deal of time to develop. The psalmist realized this when he wrote, Be still and know that I am God. The Hebrew does not suggest standing around letting your mind wander, not that kind of being still. Rather, it means let go, relax. If all this is true of other realms, responsibility is certainly applicable to the home and family. Children were not created to be jerked up, as my mother used to put it, but to be careful with gentleness, intention, and detail. They require time, lots of it.",
